ater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes.' Since there are no favorable outcomes in either lottery, the comparison is between the proportion of unfavorable vs. favorable. In Lottery A, favorable is 20% (0 Euros), unfavorable is 80%. In Lottery B, favorable is 0%, unfavorable is 100%. So in Lottery A, unfavorable > favorable (80% > 20%). In Lottery B, unfavorable > favorable (100% > 0%). Both have unfavorable > favorable. But the reason prefers the lottery that is more right-skewed — i.e., where unfavorable outcomes dominate in proportion. Since both have unfavorable dominating, we must compare the degree of dominance. In Lottery A, unfavorable is 80% vs. 20% favorable. In Lottery B, unfavorable is 100% vs. 0% favorable. The dominance in B is stronger (100% vs. 0%). Therefore, Lottery B is more right-skewed. The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, so it prefers Lottery B.",
